"[2511.15709v1] Tokenisation over Bounded Alphabets is Hard (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.15709v1)\nciteturn2463view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.15709v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 163\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 19 Nov 2025 (this version), latest version 8 Aug 2026 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:Tokenisation over Bounded Alphabets is Hard\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Violeta Kastreva , cite7†Philip Whittington , cite8†Dennis Komm , cite9†Tiago Pimentel L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Tokenisation over Bounded Alphabets is Hard, by Violeta Kastreva and Philip Whittington and Dennis Komm and Tiago Pimentel\nL14: \nL15: cite10†View PDF cite11†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Recent works have shown that tokenisation is NP-complete. However, these works assume tokenisation is applied to inputs with unboundedly large alphabets -- an unrealistic assumption, given that in practice tokenisers operate over fixed-size alphabets, such as bytes or Unicode characters.\nL17: We close this gap by analysing tokenisation over bounded $n$-ary alphabets, considering two natural variants: bottom-up tokenisation and direct tokenisation, where we must, respectively, select a sequence of merge operations or a vocabulary whose application optimally compresses a dataset. First, we note that proving hardness results for an $n$-ary alphabet proves the same results for alphabets of any larger size.\nL18: We then prove that even with binary alphabets, both variants are not only NP-complete, but admit no polynomial-time approximation scheme (unless P=NP). We further show that direct tokenisation remains NP-complete even when applied to unary alphabets. While unary alphabets may not be practically useful, this result establishes that the computational intractability of tokenisation is not an artifact of large alphabets or complex constructions, but a fundamental barrier.\nL19: Overall, our results explain why practical algorithms such as BPE and UnigramLM are heuristic, and points toward approximation algorithms being an important path going forward for tokenisation research.\nL20: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2511.15709 [cs.CL]\nL22:    | (or cite13†arXiv:2511.15709v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL23:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.15709†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Violeta Kastreva Mrs. [cite15†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Wed, 19 Nov 2025 18:59:56 UTC (69 KB)\nL28: cite5†[v2] Sat, 8 Aug 2026 14:40:40 UTC (99 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Tokenisation over Bounded Alphabets is Hard, by Violeta Kastreva and Philip Whittington and Dennis Komm and Tiago Pimentel\nL35: \nL36:   * cite10†View PDF L37:   * cite11†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite16†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite17†view license†creativecommons.org L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.CL\nL44: \nL45: cite18†< prev |   cite19†next > L46: \nL47: cite20†new | cite21†recent | cite22†2025-11 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite23†cs L52: cite24†cs.DS L53: cite25†cs.LG L54: \nL55: ### References & Citations\nL56: \nL57:   * cite26†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L58:   * cite27†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L59:   * cite28†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L60: \nL61: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL62: \nL63: ## BibTeX formatted c