: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 20 Nov 2025 (this version), latest version 8 Jan 2026 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:Liars' Bench: Evaluating Lie Detectors for Language Models\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Kieron Kretschmar (1), cite7†Walter Laurito (1 and 2), cite8†Sharan Maiya (1 and 3), cite9†Samuel Marks (4) ((1) Cadenza Labs, (2) FZI, (3) University of Cambridge, (4) Anthropic)\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Liars' Bench: Evaluating Lie Detectors for Language Models, by Kieron Kretschmar (1) and 6 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite10†View PDF cite11†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Prior work has introduced techniques for detecting when large language models (LLMs) lie, that is, generating statements they believe are false. However, these techniques are typically validated in narrow settings that do not capture the diverse lies LLMs can generate. We introduce LIARS' BENCH, a testbed consisting of 72,863 examples of lies and honest responses generated by four open-weight models across seven datasets.\nL17: Our settings capture qualitatively different types of lies and vary along two dimensions: the model's reason for lying and the object of belief targeted by the lie. Evaluating three black- and white-box lie detection techniques on LIARS' BENCH, we find that existing techniques systematically fail to identify certain types of lies, especially in settings where it's not possible to determine whether the model lied from the transcript alone.\nL18: Overall, LIARS' BENCH reveals limitations in prior techniques and provides a practical testbed for guiding progress in lie detection.\nL19: Comments:  | *Kieron Kretschmar and Walter Laurito contributed equally to this work. 10 pages, 2 figures; plus appendix. Code at cite12†this https URL†github.com and datasets at cite13†this https URL†huggingface.co Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL20: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite14†arXiv:2511.16035 [cs.CL]\nL22:    | (or cite15†arXiv:2511.16035v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL23:    | cite16†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.16035†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Walter Laurito [cite17†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Thu, 20 Nov 2025 04:29:33 UTC (507 KB)\nL28: cite5†[v2] Thu, 8 Jan 2026 21:10:47 UTC (627 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Liars' Bench: Evaluating Lie Detectors for Language Models, by Kieron Kretschmar (1) and 6 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite10†View PDF L37:   * cite11†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite18†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite19†view license†creativecommons.org L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.CL\nL44: \nL45: cite20†< prev |   cite21†next > L46: \nL47: cite22†new | cite23†recent | cite24†2025-11 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite25†cs L52: cite26†cs.AI L53: \nL54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite27†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L57:   * cite28†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L58:   * cite29†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L59: \nL60: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL61: \nL62: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL63: \nL64: [Button: ×]\nL65: \nL66: loading...\nL67: \nL68: Data provided by:\nL69: \nL70: ### Bookmark\nL71: \nL72: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL73: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL74: \nL75: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL76: \nL77: Bibliographic Explorer (cite30†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL78: \nL79: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL80: \nL81: Connected Papers (cite31†What is Connected Papers